<Base title="About the guide and blog — styleguide.fyi" description="How styleguide.fyi develops practical coding advice, publishes articles, and distinguishes original writing from captured public style guides." canonicalPath="/about" markdownUrl={null}>
	<article class="about-page"><h1>Advice you can inspect.</h1><p>styleguide.fyi is a public coding guide for people and agents. It pairs proposed rules with reasons, examples, and independent reviewer assessments.</p><h2>What the blog adds</h2><p>The <a href="/blog">blog</a> works through the decisions behind the rules: choosing conventions, reviewing changes, and handling trade-offs. Articles are published under the styleguide.fyi name. Writing and implementation may be assisted by AI; an article is not an independent Astra or Fable assessment.</p><h2>Sources and corrections</h2><p>Original articles link to relevant rules and primary sources. The <a href="/library">captured library</a> is separate: those documents belong to their credited authors and retain their source and license information. A citation is not an endorsement by that author.</p><p>The source for each article is available in the <a href="https://github.com/steventsao/styleguidefyi/tree/main/src/content/blog">public repository</a>. To suggest a correction, <a href="https://github.com/steventsao/styleguidefyi/issues">open an issue</a> with the article URL and the specific claim or example.</p><h2>Dates and changes</h2><p>Articles show their publication date and, after a substantive revision, an update date. Guide reviews track the wording they assessed. Editing a rule can make a previous review stale; it does not silently renew that review.</p><p><a href="/newsletter">Join the newsletter launch list</a> or <a href="/rss.xml">follow the blog via RSS</a>.</p></article>
</Base>
